# Sanity MCP Maintenance Workflow (Eleventy + Sanity)

Use this workflow when adding content, pages, or small features. Prioritize low-risk, high-confidence changes over refactors.

## Operating Principle

- Always validate content contracts before editing templates.
- Prefer schema and query corrections over patching issues in frontend rendering logic.
- Keep maintenance steps lightweight and repeatable.

## Required Sanity MCP Sequence

1. Read current schema before any query or content assumptions.
2. Audit existing GROQ used by affected `web/_data/*.js` loaders.
3. Validate queried fields exist, are correctly typed, and match template expectations.
4. Identify missing validation or weak constraints in touched schema fields.
5. Propose minimum-scope fixes with explicit risk and effort.

## When Adding or Updating a Feature

- Reuse existing document types, references, and object schemas first.
- Fetch only required fields in GROQ projections; avoid over-fetching.
- Confirm singleton documents exist and are complete where required.
- Verify portable text, image, and slug fields expected by templates are present.
- Prefer content-model fixes over custom fallback logic in templates.

## Sanity Quality Checks (Run Before Release)

- Missing slugs in routable content (posts, books, events, series).
- Missing or weak SEO/social fields used by meta tags.
- Missing image metadata required for rendering and accessibility where applicable.
- Null or malformed values for date/schedule fields used in filtering/sorting.
- References that resolve to missing or unpublished documents.

## Maintenance Cadence (Low Overhead)

- On each maintenance cycle, run a focused Sanity MCP audit for only touched content types.
- Keep audits scoped to current work; do not run broad system refactors.
- Document assumptions and unresolved content debt as follow-up items.

## Decision Rules

- If issue can be fixed in schema validation, do that first.
- If issue is query shape mismatch, fix GROQ projection/filter before template logic.
- If issue is isolated content data quality, patch documents instead of code refactors.
- Escalate to structural changes only when repeated failures prove current model is insufficient.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Editing frontend templates first when data contract is uncertain.
- Adding custom transformation layers for one-off content inconsistencies.
- Expanding query payload "just in case."
- Mixing migration/refactor work into a small feature release.
